# 0020. `/handoff`: pass the thread to another model

- Status: Accepted (owner, 2026-11-25; revised after Codex's critique the same day)
- Date: 2026-11-25

[0008](0008-slash-commands-and-mentions.adr) lists `/handoff <id>`: "send a summary of the
thread to `<id>` and make it default". Useful when one model hits its limit
([0014](0014-usage-plan-limits.adr)) or another is better placed to carry on. This ADR
decides who writes the summary, what it contains and what changes after.

## Technical details
- **`/handoff <id> [note]`**, three steps, all read-only even when either side is the
  `writer`:
  1. **Summary.** The summarizer is the current default (with `default = "all"`, the
     writer; otherwise `/handoff <id> from <summarizer>`). A model can only summarize what
     it saw, so its prompt carries the thread itself: the `/log` rendering
     ([0024](0024-log-command.adr)) of this thread, oldest messages dropped to fit the
     summarizer's window, with the instruction: summarize for a colleague who takes over
     (goal, decisions, state of the work, open questions, files touched, next step), plus
     `note`. The summarizer may never have received parts of that log (other models'
     exchanges, their tool lines, errors), so before anything is sent the chat names both
     sides and what is new to the summarizer, and asks: `claude summarizes for codex;
     claude has not seen 12 entries (llama, codex) — send them to claude? y/n`. "Unseen" is
     checked against the exact rendered text being sent, entry by entry (message, tool
     line, error): any entry that did not come from or go to the summarizer counts. No
     unseen entry: no question.
  2. **Dispatch.** The summary goes to `<id>` as `handoff from <summarizer>: <summary>`
     plus the note; `<id>` replies with what it understood and its first step. Only the
     summary crosses to `<id>`'s provider, never other participants' raw replies.
  3. **Default.** Only after `<id>`'s reply completes does `default` become `<id>`, the same
     way `/model use <id>` does it ([0011](0011-hot-model-registry.adr)): the live registry
     changes at once, then `models.toml` is written; if that write fails the chat says so
     and the new default stays active for this session only. `writer` never changes: the chat says
     `codex is now the default; it is read-only (writer: claude)` when that applies.
- **Durable and unambiguous** ([0010](0010-filesystem-event-log.adr)): a `handoff` intent
  event, synced before step 1, pins both participants' identities. Before step 2 the
  target is rechecked: disabled or repointed since (identity differs) settles the handoff
  `blocked` with that reason. Every handoff ends in one `settle`: `complete`, `failed`
  (either turn failed), `interrupted` (`Esc`, as 0010 names it), or `blocked`;
  `cancelled` stays a delivery status. Only `complete` changes the
  default. `turn`/`parent` link the summary turn, the dispatch and the reply to the intent.
  In the chat it is one collapsed line that `Enter` expands (0008).
- **`raw` instead of a summary**: `/handoff <id> raw [n]` sends the last `n` messages
  (default 20, fewer if they don't fit `<id>`'s window) verbatim, other participants'
  replies included, so they reach `<id>`'s provider. It asks first: `send 20 messages
  (claude, codex) to llama at http://… ? y/n`. For when the summarizer is out of quota.
- **Free-model targets**: the handoff message is appended to `<id>`'s identity-bound
  history ([0005](0005-openai-compatible-models.adr)) like any delivered message; nothing is
  reset, and `/resume` rebuilds it the same way.
- Consequences: switching who leads takes one command and keeps the reasoning, not just
  the last message; nothing silently changes the default or leaks raw replies. Costs: a
  summary turn (tokens, time), summaries lose detail (the full thread stays in `/log`),
  and the summarizer's prompt carries the thread, so a long thread costs a full window.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Alternatives:
  - roundtable builds the summary itself from the transcript (no model turn): free and
    instant, but a list of messages is not a handover; kept as the `raw` fallback.
  - Moving the writer role along with the default: a model that never edited here would
    start editing without the owner choosing it.
